WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.740
How can readers share while

2
00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:04.780
writers stay exclusive? Multiple readers

3
00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:06.480
can safely inspect the protected

4
00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:08.140
data together, but a writer

5
00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:10.540
needs exclusive access against both

6
00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:13.180
readers and other writers. The

7
00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:16.140
reader-preference semaphore solution separates data

8
00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:17.860
access from protection of the

9
00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:18.460
reader count.

10
00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:20.720
The mutex protects the shared

11
00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:23.120
readcount variable, not the entire

12
00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:25.180
duration of reading. The semaphore

13
00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:27.000
called write protects admission to

14
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.840
the database. A writer waits

15
00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:30.860
on that semaphore, writes alone,

16
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:32.760
then signals it when finished.

17
00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:35.260
Now start with no writer.

18
00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:37.760
Reader R one takes mutex,

19
00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:39.900
increments readcount from zero to

20
00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:41.380
one, and as the first

21
00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:42.920
reader waits on write. It

22
00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:45.220
then releases mutex and performs

23
00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:46.620
its reading while holding the

24
00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:48.780
group database exclusion against writers.

25
00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:51.900
Reader R two takes mutex

26
00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:54.080
and increments readcount to two.

27
00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:55.640
Because it is not the

28
00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:57.240
first reader, it does not

29
00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:59.500
acquire write again. It releases

30
00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:01.420
mutex and joins R one

31
00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:03.100
in the database, so the

32
00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:05.160
two reading activities can overlap.

33
00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:07.580
A writer arriving now waits

34
00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:09.600
on write and blocks. That

35
00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:10.800
does not stop the current

36
00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:12.340
readers from finishing their reads.

37
00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:14.560
The data remains protected because

38
00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:16.100
the reader group still controls

39
00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:17.280
the database semaphore.

40
00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:19.420
When R one leaves, it

41
00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:21.900
takes mutex and decrements readcount

42
00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:23.800
from two to one. It

43
00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:24.960
is not the last reader,

44
00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:26.960
so it leaves write unavailable

45
00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:28.780
to the writer. Releasing the

46
00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:30.900
reader-count mutex does not imply

47
00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:32.560
that the database is empty.

48
00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:36.520
R two eventually decrements readcount

49
00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:38.380
to zero and signals write

50
00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:39.820
because it is the last

51
00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:41.840
reader. Under the blocking handoff

52
00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:43.540
model, the writer can become

53
00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:45.220
ready with the database permit

54
00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:47.540
reserved. Dispatch is still a

55
00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:50.120
scheduling decision, not an instantaneous

56
00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:51.500
consequence of the signal.

57
00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:54.600
The writer then executes exclusively

58
00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:56.840
and signals when done. Notice

59
00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:58.580
the two different protections: short

60
00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:00.820
mutex sections serialize updates to

61
00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:02.840
readcount, while write controls the

62
00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:04.180
reader group versus a writer.

63
00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:06.200
Holding mutex during every whole

64
00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:08.540
read would unnecessarily serialize readers.

65
00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:11.140
In this reader-preference version, an

66
00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:12.680
ongoing stream of new readers

67
00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:14.340
may keep readcount above zero

68
00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:15.980
and postpone a writer indefinitely.

69
00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:18.680
Exclusion can remain correct while

70
00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:21.380
writer fairness fails. Writer preference

71
00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:22.880
or a fair arrival policy

72
00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:24.360
needs a different admission design.

73
00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:26.060
Things to take note of.

74
00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:28.420
The first reader acquires write,

75
00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:29.860
the last reader releases it,

76
00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:31.740
and every readcount change is

77
00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:34.360
protected by mutex. State that

78
00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:35.820
this is reader preference and

79
00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:38.300
explain its starvation caveat rather

80
00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:40.140
than claiming that correct exclusion

81
00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:41.220
guarantees fairness.
